\draw[->] (-2.6,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$p$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$q$};
\node[text=blue, right] at (3.4,1.4) {level contours of $v$};
\node[text=red, right] at (3.4,0.7) {level contours of $u$};
\node[text=blue, right] at (3.4,0.0) {$\to$ direction of decreasing $u$};
\node[right] at (3.4,-0.7) {crossings are at right angles};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{\strong{Interlude 2: Cauchy’s Theorem.} #{\oint_C fe^{\phi x}\,dz = 0} if #{f} and #{\phi} are analytic in the closed domain bounded by #{C}. In particular, suppose #{C_1} and #{C_2} both run from #{z_1} to #{z_2}; then #{\int_{C_1} = \int_{C_2}} if #{f, \phi} are analytic between the curves.}

\ul{
\li{\p{\strong{(a)} \em{Case when #{\operatorname{Im}(\phi) = v} is constant on #{C}.} Let #{v = v_0 \in \mathbb{R}} constant on #{C}. Then #{C} is a steepest descent/ascent curve for #{u}. Let’s parameterise #{C}: #{z = z(t) = p(t) + iq(t)}, #{a < t < b} (#{t \in \mathbb{R}}, #{-\infty \leq a, b \leq \infty}). Then}
##{I(x) = e^{ixv_0}\int_a^b e^{xu}\operatorname{Re}\Big( f\frac{dz}{dt} \Big)\,dt + ie^{ixv_0}\int_a^b e^{xu}\operatorname{Im}\Big( f\frac{dz}{dt} \Big)\,dt.}
\p{Both integrals are real and can be attacked by Laplace’s method/Watson’s Lemma.}}
\li{\p{\strong{(b)} \em{Deforming #{C} to a steepest descent contour of #{u}.}}
\ul{
\li{If #{v} is not constant on #{C}, deform #{C} to a curve on which it is! (Using Cauchy’s theorem.)}
\li{If #{v} is identical on #{C}’s endpoints this might work: deform #{C} to #{C'} on which #{v = v_1} constant, reducing to case (a) since #{v} is constant on #{C'} (not #{C}).}
\li{But this is not always possible, and certainly doesn’t work if #{v} takes different values on the 2 endpoints.}
}
\p{So then we deform #{C} to a curve that follows several constant-#{v} curves joined up by short bridging curves (on which #{v} is not constant).}
\tex{\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\usepackage{tikz}\usetikzlibrary{patterns,calc}}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\draw[blue, dashed] (-0.6,-0.6) -- (1.6,1.6);
\draw[red, dashed] (2.2,1.5) .. controls (3.2,0.4) .. (5.6,0.2) -- (6.6,0.1);
\draw[thick] (0,0) .. controls (1.5,-2.0) and (3.5,-1.0) .. (5.2,0.2);
\node[below] at (3.6,-1.2) {$C$};
\draw[gray, thick, ->] (0,0) -- (1.3,1.3) node[midway, above left] {$C_1$};
\draw[gray, thick, ->] (1.3,1.3) -- (2.3,1.5) node[midway, above=4pt] {$C_2$};
\draw[gray, thick, ->] (2.3,1.5) .. controls (3.2,0.5) .. (5.2,0.2);
\node[gray] at (3.9,0.9) {$C_3$};
\fill (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (5.2,0.2) circle (1.5pt);
\node[blue, above left] at (-0.1,0.05) {$v = v_1$};
\node[red, below right] at (5.2,0.1) {$v = v_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}}
\p{Then #{\int_C = \int_{C_1} + \int_{C_2} + \int_{C_3}}, where we calculate #{\int_{C_1}} and #{\int_{C_3}} with (a), and show #{\int_{C_2}} contributes very little.}
\p{#{u} is maximally increasing/decreasing on #{v}-constant curves. We select curves so that #{u} is decreasing as we move away from the endpoints. Why? Assuming #{\nabla u \neq 0}, Laplace’s method tells us that the absolute maximum dominates the asymptotic expansion, and these will correspond to the endpoints!}}
}

\transclude{AM0W}

\transclude{AM0X}

\ul{
\li{\p{\strong{(c)} \em{Saddle points.}}
\ul{
\li{Laplace’s method tells us that the absolute maxima of #{u} dominate #{I(x)}. This might occur at endpoints or internal points.}
\li{Note if #{f = 0} at the endpoints, they don’t contribute to the asymptotic expansion.}
\li{If #{C} goes from #{\infty} to #{\infty} in #{\mathbb{C}}, there are no endpoints.}
\li{Let’s now look at incorporating “internal” maxima of #{u} into the procedure.}
}
\p{\strong{Interlude 3.}}
\ul{
\li{\strong{(i)} If #{u} has a critical point in the #{(p, q)} plane, then #{\nabla u = 0} there, but #{v} has a critical point there as well. Follows from the Cauchy–Riemann equations.}
\li{\strong{(ii)} Critical points of #{u} (and #{v}) are saddles in the #{(p, q)} plane. First, #{\nabla^2u = 0} (by the Cauchy–Riemann equations). Like the maximum modulus principle: for #{u} to have a max or min, we would need #{u_{pp}u_{qq} - u_{pq}^2 > 0}, but this is #{-u_{pp}^2 - u_{pq}^2 > 0}, impossible. So critical points of #{u} are saddles (same for #{v}).}
\li{\strong{(iii)} If #{z_0 = (p_0, q_0)} is a critical point of #{u} and #{v}, then #{\frac{d\phi}{dz} = 0} there (Cauchy–Riemann equations).}
}
\p{Suppose #{(p_0, q_0)} is a simple saddle of #{u} and #{v} (#{\frac{d^2\phi}{dz^2} \neq 0}).}
\tex{\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\usepackage{tikz}\usetikzlibrary{patterns,calc}}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$p$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$q$};
\draw[dashed, gray] (1.3,0.6) -- (3.3,2.6);
\draw[dashed, gray] (1.3,2.6) -- (3.3,0.6);
\draw[red, thick] (1.0,1.35) -- (3.6,1.85);
\draw[red, thick] (2.162,2.75) -- (2.438,0.45);
\draw[red, thick, ->] (1.0,1.35) -- (1.45,1.437);
\draw[red, thick, ->] (1.55,1.456) -- (1.95,1.533);
\draw[red, thick, ->] (3.6,1.85) -- (3.15,1.763);
\draw[red, thick, ->] (3.05,1.744) -- (2.65,1.667);
\draw[red, thick, ->] (2.282,1.75) -- (2.24,2.1);
\draw[red, thick, ->] (2.216,2.3) -- (2.168,2.7);
\draw[red, thick, ->] (2.318,1.45) -- (2.36,1.1);
\draw[red, thick, ->] (2.384,0.9) -- (2.432,0.5);
\fill (2.3,1.6) circle (1.6pt);
\draw[dotted] (2.3,1.6) -- (2.3,0) node[below] {$p_0$};
\draw[dotted] (2.3,1.6) -- (0,1.6) node[left] {$q_0$};
\draw[red, ->] (3.2,-0.55) node[below, text=red, align=center] {\small want this curve\\ \small for Laplace} .. controls (3.9,0.2) .. (3.65,1.6);
\node[text=gray, right] at (4.9,2.3) {-- -- const.\ $u$};
\node[text=red, right] at (4.9,1.7) {$\to$ increasing $u$ directions};
\node[text=red, right] at (4.9,1.1) {-- also are const.\ $v$ curves};
\end{tikzpicture}}
\p{If we are to deform #{C} to pass through #{(p_0, q_0)}, we select the constant-#{v} curve on which #{u} is decreasing away from that point on either side (“top of the hill”): we want this curve for Laplace.}
\p{\em{Procedure:}}
\ul{
\li{Find the locations of all critical points by #{d\phi /dz = 0}.}
\li{If possible, deform #{C} so that it passes through the saddle points.}
\li{The contribution from a saddle point can be calculated from local information at that saddle point #{z_0}.}
}
\p{In particular, we can linearise #{C'} (treat it like a straight tangent line through #{z_0}). Then we can parameterise the curve more simply: #{z(t) = z_0 + te^{i\theta}}, #{\theta} constant. Along #{v}-constant curves, #{\phi(z) - \phi(z_0) \in \mathbb{R}}, so for a simple saddle,}
##{\phi(z) - \phi(z_0) = \frac{1}{2}\phi''(z_0)t^2e^{2i\theta} + \cdots = \frac{1}{2}|\phi''(z_0)|t^2e^{2i\theta + i\alpha},}
\p{where #{\alpha = \arg(\phi''(z_0))}.
So #{\sin(2\theta + \alpha) = 0 \Rightarrow \theta = -\frac{\alpha}{2} + \frac{n\pi}{2}}, #{n = 0, 1, 2, 3}; #{n = 1, 3} are the steepest descent rays.}
\ul{
\li{Put #{z(t)} into #{I(x)} and use Laplace’s method for #{n = 1, 3}.}
\li{For non-simple saddles, see p. 37 in Stuart.}
}}
}

\transclude{AM0Y}

\p{\strong{The Asymptotics of the Airy Function}}

\p{The Airy equation: #{y'' - xy = 0}.}

\ul{
\li{Provides the canonical description of turning points and wave transitions.}
\li{One solution is #{\operatorname{Ai}(x) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{\frac{1}{3}it^3 + xit}\,dt}. [The other solution is #{\operatorname{Bi}(x)}.]}
\li{We will be interested in the large #{x} asymptotics.}
}

\ul{
\li{\p{\strong{(a)} #{x > 0} large. First substitute #{s = t/\sqrt{x}}:}
##{\operatorname{Ai} = \frac{\sqrt{x}}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{ix^{3/2}(\frac{1}{3}s^3 + s)}\,ds.}
\ul{
\li{Stationary phase doesn’t work (stationary points #{\in \mathbb{C} \setminus \mathbb{R}}); try steepest descent, #{s = z \in \mathbb{C}}.}
\li{\p{Note: the new contour in #{\mathbb{C}} must have #{\operatorname{Im}(z^3) > 0} far from the origin; we need to go to #{\infty} in the shaded regions (#{0 < \arg z < \frac{\pi}{3}} and #{\frac{2\pi}{3} < \arg z < \pi}).}
\tex{\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\usepackage{tikz}\usetikzlibrary{patterns,calc}}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\fill[gray!25] (0,0) -- (2.6,0) arc (0:60:2.6) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!25] (0,0) -- (120:2.6) arc (120:180:2.6) -- cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (60:2.7);
\draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (120:2.7);
\draw[->] (-3,0) -- (3.2,0) node[right] {$p$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,2.9) node[above] {$q$};
\draw (0.75,0) arc (0:60:0.75);
\node at (30:1.1) {$\frac{\pi}{3}$};
\draw (120:0.75) arc (120:180:0.75);
\node at (150:1.1) {$\frac{\pi}{3}$};
\node[right, align=left] at (3.4,1.5) {need to go to $\infty$\\ in the shaded regions};
\end{tikzpicture}}}
\li{Find saddles: #{\phi = i(\frac{1}{3}z^3 + z)}, #{\phi' = i(z^2 + 1) = 0 \Rightarrow z = \pm i}.}
\li{\p{Find constant-#{v} curves: #{\phi = u + iv}, #{z = p + iq}:}
##{u = \tfrac{1}{3}q^3 - p^2q - q, \qquad v = \tfrac{1}{3}p^3 - pq^2 + p = \text{const}.}
\p{#{v = 0} on the upper saddle point #{\Rightarrow} #{p = 0} (steepest ascent for #{u}) or #{q = +\sqrt{\frac{1}{3}p^2 + 1}} (steepest descent for #{u}), which goes to #{\infty} at angle #{\sim \pi /6}.}}
}
\tex{\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\usepackage{tikz}\usetikzlibrary{patterns,calc}}{\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (-3,0) -- (3.2,0) node[right] {$p$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,2.2) node[above] {$q$};
\draw[dashed, domain=-2.5:2.5, samples=50] plot (\x, {sqrt(\x *\x /3 + 1)*0.8});
\draw[blue, thick, ->] (-2.3,0) -- (-2.3,{sqrt(2.3*2.3/3+1)*0.8});
\draw[blue, thick, ->, domain=-2.3:2.3, samples=50] plot (\x, {sqrt(\x *\x /3 + 1)*0.8});
\draw[blue, thick, ->] (2.3,{sqrt(2.3*2.3/3+1)*0.8}) -- (2.3,0);
\draw[thick, ->] (-2.3,0) -- (1.5,0);
\node[below] at (1.3,0) {$C$};
\node[below] at (-2.3,0) {$-R$};
\node[below] at (2.3,0) {$R$};
\node[blue, left] at (-2.3,0.6) {$C_1$};
\node[blue, above] at (1.2,1.15) {$C_2$};
\node[blue, right] at (2.3,0.6) {$C_3$};
\fill (0,0.8) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above right] at (0,0.85) {$i$};
\end{tikzpicture}}
\p{#{\int_C = \int_{C_1} + \int_{C_2} + \int_{C_3}}; as #{R \to \infty}, #{\int_{C_1}, \int_{C_3} \to 0} because the integrand #{\sim e^{-R^3}}. Now apply the formula for the saddle contribution to #{\int_{C_2}}, with #{\phi_0 = \phi(i) = -\frac{2}{3}}:}
##{\operatorname{Ai} = \int_C \sim \int_{C_2} \sim \frac{\sqrt{x}}{2\pi}e^{x^{3/2}\phi_0}\sqrt{\frac{-2\pi}{x^{3/2}\phi_0''}} \sim \frac{1}{2\sqrt{\pi}}x^{-1/4}e^{-\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}} \quad \text{as } x \to \infty.}}
\li{\p{\strong{(b)} #{x < 0} and large. First #{s = t/\sqrt{-x}}:}
##{\operatorname{Ai}(x) = \frac{\sqrt{-x}}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^\infty \exp\big[ i(-x)^{3/2}\big( \tfrac{1}{3}s^3 - s \big) \big]\,ds.}
\p{Stationary phase will work: #{\phi = \frac{1}{3}s^3 - s}, so #{s = \pm 1} are points of stationary phase. So}
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Ai} &\sim \frac{\sqrt{-x}}{2\pi}\Big[ \sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{-i(-x)^{3/2}\phi''(1)}}e^{i(-x)^{3/2}\phi(1)} + \sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{-i(-x)^{3/2}\phi''(-1)}}e^{i(-x)^{3/2}\phi(-1)} \Big] \\
&\sim \frac{(-x)^{-1/4}}{\sqrt{\pi}}\cos\Big( \frac{2}{3}(-x)^{3/2} - \frac{\pi}{4} \Big) \quad \text{as } x \to -\infty.
\end{aligned}}
